{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED at exact clean pushed head 8286c791706469271dfc6a90100df4f07885cfbd, artifact 25e2fa64 at spike/querysandbox. Two narrow items, both in the honesty of the write-up rather than in the mechanism. Read the first paragraph before the rest, because the headline is not the blocker.\n\nTHE DESIGN CONCLUSION HOLDS AND IS NOT IN QUESTION. The SQLite authorizer is a real boundary, the pinned ncruces binding supports it, and nothing here argues for changing the driver or narrowing the SQL surface. Spikes one and three are not blocked by this verdict and should proceed on that basis. What I am asking for is two sentences and one line of code.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY, CHECKED AT THE MECHANISM AND NOT ONLY AT THE TESTS. applicationAuthorizer admits exactly four things: AUTH_SELECT, AUTH_RECURSIVE, AUTH_READ for schema \"main\" limited to inventory and inventory_summary by name, and eight named functions. Everything else reaches default and denies — writes, DDL, PRAGMA, transaction control, ATTACH/DETACH, virtual tables, savepoints, and any action code that does not exist yet. That is default-deny by construction rather than by enumeration, which is the property that matters and the one I had claimed publicly when the external reviewer asked about prompt injection. It holds.\n\nThe defence in depth around it is well ordered and I checked the ordering rather than assuming it. query_only is set BEFORE the authorizer is installed, which is why no application PRAGMA exception is needed and why the PRAGMA row of your table can honestly say \"None\" for secondary enforcement. DEFENSIVE on, TRUSTED_SCHEMA off, ENABLE_LOAD_EXTENSION off, LIMIT_ATTACHED zero, read-only open, and a prepared-statement ReadOnly check with a single-statement tail check. Extension loading is refused twice over: by the function allowlist and by the config.\n\nMUTATION, RUN BY ME AGAINST THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN REPRODUCED FROM THE REPORT. I replaced the authorizer's default arm with AUTH_OK. Three tests go red: TestApplicationQueryRefusals on both PRAGMA subtests, TestAuthorizerMutationProof, and TestAuthorizerIsDefaultDeny. Restoring makes them pass and the tree is byte-clean afterwards. Your two in-test mutation proofs are also genuine — they open connections with installAuthorizer false and with the deadline decision disabled and require the named assertions to fail — and TestAuthorizerIsDefaultDeny walking every action code from 1 to AUTH_RECURSIVE plus an invented 10_000 is the strongest single test in the spike.\n\nGATES, from a clean detached checkout at the exact head: gofmt silent, git diff --check clean, go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./spike/querysandbox -count=10 ok at 0.781s, go test -race ./spike/querysandbox -count=1 ok at 2.354s, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 33 packages ok.\n\nFIRST CHANGE REQUESTED, AND I MEASURED IT RATHER THAN REASONING ABOUT IT. The MaxResultBytes check runs after a row has been fully materialised: Query builds every column value into the row, sums rowBytes, and only then tests bytesUsed+rowBytes \u003e MaxResultBytes. So the cap bounds what is returned, which your test correctly proves, but not what is read into memory to decide.\n\nI probed it. A four-column row of 60 KiB values, queried through a sandbox with the authorizer disabled to isolate the wrapper's accounting, returns zero rows and Truncated true — correct — while allocating 264,352 bytes, 32.3 times the 8 KiB cap. That is with four columns. The engine's own limits in this file allow LIMIT_COLUMN 32 at LIMIT_LENGTH 64 KiB, so the ceiling is about 2 MiB, roughly 256 times the cap.\n\nThe README's gap list says the byte count \"does not account for Go slice, string, allocator, or JSON-encoding overhead, so it is not a memory quota\". That sentence is true and still misleads, because it names the small term. The dominant unaccounted quantity is SQLite value bytes themselves, admitted before the check that rejects them. Someone sizing a read pool from this page would budget 8 KiB per query and be wrong by two orders of magnitude, and this spike exists precisely to inform that kind of sizing. Add a sentence to \"What this does not close\" naming the per-row overshoot and its magnitude. I am not asking you to change the check; deferring the cap to before materialisation is a production design question, not a spike repair.\n\nSECOND CHANGE REQUESTED, one line. columnValue ends in panic(\"unreachable SQLite datatype\"). It is unreachable against the pinned driver today, so this is not a live defect. But it is the one place in this file that answers an unexpected value by crashing rather than refusing, in a component whose entire thesis is that unknown inputs deny. A panic here takes the process down rather than failing the query closed, and the spike's own principle — stated in the authorizer's default arm and in the README's \"an unknown future authorizer action therefore denies\" — says the opposite. Return an error.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The spike sits under spike/ beside the seven production layers and changes none of their contracts. It does not import into the kernel, teach it database meaning, or touch the resident or application profile. Your architecture paragraph is accurate and docs/reference/architecture.md needs no change in this head. I checked that the gaps you list are narrowings of a future production contract rather than pressure on the kernel, which is the stop rule request 466a084e carried; none of them push the other way.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary is agent-authored SQL against a projection, and it is fail-closed at the authorizer, at the connection configuration, and at the statement shape. The resource bounds are honestly labelled as elapsed-time cancellation rather than a step budget. The two unresolved items are the memory overshoot above and the panic above; neither is exploitable through the authorizer, both are worth stating rather than leaving for a production reader to discover.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The engine limits are individually justified, the wrapper is close to minimal for what it must prove, and the README's table earns its length by separating primary from secondary enforcement per class.\n\nWHAT I WOULD HAVE SAID IF THE GAP LIST WERE NOT THERE. This spike is unusually candid about what it does not close, and that is most of its value — a spike that only reports what passed cannot inform a design decision. Both requested changes are in that section rather than against it. Fix them and I will approve at the next exact head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:25e2fa64d7b51b00bc3a2f5a87636558e88bef31","head":"8286c791706469271dfc6a90100df4f07885cfbd","verdict":"changes-requested"}}